{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1150 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "8a032e083b74e69091d1bc0e9cdc7587feca0a5f3f2d90f9e9c436e4b707a098" :edb/db-before-t 1149 :edb/db-after-t 1150 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534462 50 #inst "2026-10-07T06:06:37.386Z" 13194139534462 true] [17592186064078 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289577 13194139534462 true] [17592186064078 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289584 13194139534462 true] [17592186064078 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289598 13194139534462 true] [17592186064185 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289591 13194139534462 true] [17592186064185 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289605 13194139534462 true] [17592186289577 1017 #edb/ref 17592186289578 13194139534462 true] [17592186289577 1089 "q-103437" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289577 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534462 true] [17592186289577 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011175/assets/fb61c048eb7eac87f82c53717f8f67251b59610817ddf1f267e80543c0c86959.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=-1$\n- $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=-1$\n- $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=-1$" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289577 1113 #uuid "a63bb70d-731b-554c-adf7-37f9033998a8" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289577 1145 "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ In this case, the graph of the function has a horizontal tangent line at $x=-1,$ and therefore, ${f}^{′}(-1)=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a critical point at $x=-1$ since each stationary point is a critical point.\n\n- Statement III is false. The function has a local minimum at $x=-1$ since $f(-1)\\le f(x)$ for all $x$ close enough to $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, statements I and II are true, while statement III is false." 13194139534462 true] [17592186289578 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289579 13194139534462 true] [17592186289578 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289580 13194139534462 true] [17592186289578 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289581 13194139534462 true] [17592186289578 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289582 13194139534462 true] [17592186289578 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289583 13194139534462 true] [17592186289578 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534462 true] [17592186289578 1041 "selection" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289578 1104 #edb/ref 17592186289582 13194139534462 true] [17592186289578 1110 #uuid "6d7afee5-9159-52f5-88ce-0aa6c2474ac5" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289579 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289579 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534462 true] [17592186289579 1111 #uuid "2d2800e2-2d27-5622-a6a1-f5992cc92489" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289580 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289580 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534462 true] [17592186289580 1111 #uuid "70d1fc46-bc8c-5ff3-988a-209dc0f6ad30" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289581 1034 "III only" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289581 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534462 true] [17592186289581 1111 #uuid "6c0a2aff-d31f-5dc6-9481-56985c8e3b07" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289582 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289582 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534462 true] [17592186289582 1111 #uuid "6202b5e7-d6f4-55ac-9284-153f48291dbe" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289583 1034 "II only" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289583 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534462 true] [17592186289583 1111 #uuid "fd0048b5-4130-5d93-bc47-76c1f1c47d63" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289584 1017 #edb/ref 17592186289585 13194139534462 true] [17592186289584 1089 "q-103438" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289584 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534462 true] [17592186289584 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011175/assets/5bbb0cacbe8dca2facfd49e757ba9b7aa40ee91ae820f001c72d637186d57b79.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=-1$\n- $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=0$\n- $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x=1$" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289584 1113 #uuid "d69f67de-ee43-5100-8b00-e925c0e6171b" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289584 1145 "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function has a critical point (a corner) at $x=-1.$ In this case,  ${f}^{′}(-1)$ does not exist.\n\n- Statement II is true. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ In this case, the graph of the function has a horizontal tangent line at $x=0,$ and therefore, ${f}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function has a local minimum at $x=1$ since $f(1)\\le f(x)$ for all $x$ close enough to $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, all three statements are true." 13194139534462 true] [17592186289585 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289586 13194139534462 true] [17592186289585 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289587 13194139534462 true] [17592186289585 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289588 13194139534462 true] [17592186289585 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289589 13194139534462 true] [17592186289585 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289590 13194139534462 true] [17592186289585 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534462 true] [17592186289585 1041 "selection" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289585 1104 #edb/ref 17592186289586 13194139534462 true] [17592186289585 1110 #uuid "91463e95-1331-5fc1-a1c3-491eac483ed0" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289586 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289586 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534462 true] [17592186289586 1111 #uuid "63273267-c257-5c62-8e89-2b50792f1d3d" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289587 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289587 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534462 true] [17592186289587 1111 #uuid "2042c9b3-c29e-5782-8171-8b3be12e32c9" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289588 1034 "III only" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289588 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534462 true] [17592186289588 1111 #uuid "3cc3a8fc-b5aa-59b2-a411-b1f71fb7ac54" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289589 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289589 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534462 true] [17592186289589 1111 #uuid "cd24d442-905c-56e7-b091-7fab665406c3" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289590 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289590 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534462 true] [17592186289590 1111 #uuid "1bbeab63-f015-57e1-85f1-08bd11c5c6a2" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289591 1017 #edb/ref 17592186289592 13194139534462 true] [17592186289591 1089 "q-47915" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289591 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534462 true] [17592186289591 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011175/assets/748abf694eac3c4e59fd09d3bc48648fe30e46561f536f9f9a16eac7eb526558.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above. What are the $x$-coordinates of all the critical points?" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289591 1113 #uuid "6390010c-520d-5f4d-8e9b-7ad97c318ba0" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289591 1145 "The critical points of $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ where ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ doesn't exist. Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal or where the tangent does not exist, respectively.\n\nIn the given graph, we can see that\n\n- at any point between  $x=1$ and $x=5$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ and\n\n- at $x=1$ and at $x=5$  there are sharp corners, hence ${f}^{′}(1)$ and ${f}^{′}(5)$ don't exist.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011175/assets/48b786a6b9c270336ce507525acf2503439b2424dba59ed9ea200830a79dd828.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, all numbers in the closed interval $[1,5]$ are critical points for $f(x).$" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289592 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289593 13194139534462 true] [17592186289592 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289594 13194139534462 true] [17592186289592 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289595 13194139534462 true] [17592186289592 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289596 13194139534462 true] [17592186289592 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289597 13194139534462 true] [17592186289592 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534462 true] [17592186289592 1041 "selection" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289592 1104 #edb/ref 17592186289597 13194139534462 true] [17592186289592 1110 #uuid "c0f31a4d-e408-58d2-9646-d443d0447989" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289593 1034 "$x=1$ and $x=5$ only" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289593 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534462 true] [17592186289593 1111 #uuid "6854c36a-035a-52b7-8473-c146e56038ec" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289594 1034 "$x=5$ only" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289594 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534462 true] [17592186289594 1111 #uuid "63f22c98-e5ea-5b29-b287-7fced162cf70" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289595 1034 "$x=1$ only" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289595 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534462 true] [17592186289595 1111 #uuid "c34d4ce2-fd35-5755-ae97-2330aa24642e" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289596 1034 "None" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289596 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534462 true] [17592186289596 1111 #uuid "a045385b-5e56-5adc-83f4-e625d79c4785" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289597 1034 "All points in the closed interval $[1,5]$" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289597 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534462 true] [17592186289597 1111 #uuid "46e95f5f-eb72-5348-bd74-1fd5c7cb77d7" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289598 1017 #edb/ref 17592186289599 13194139534462 true] [17592186289598 1089 "q-47918" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289598 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534462 true] [17592186289598 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011175/assets/4a1843255ea718116bd2daa07f1375a3fb277c47ec8503fec90da824cd714d0b.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. Given that $f(x)$ is defined on the interval $x\\in [-2,10],$ which points represent the global maximum of $f(x)$ ?" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289598 1113 #uuid "126dc5db-7aa0-5e31-885e-35517971a655" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289598 1145 "The global extrema of a function are the lowest or highest points of the function's graph over its entire domain.\n\nThe lowest point is the global minimum, whereas the highest point is the global maximum.\n\nAccording to the graph, the highest point is $D,$ and so this point represents the global maximum of $f(x).$ \n\n**Note:** The point $B$ is not a global maximum, it's a local maximum." 13194139534462 true] [17592186289599 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289600 13194139534462 true] [17592186289599 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289601 13194139534462 true] [17592186289599 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289602 13194139534462 true] [17592186289599 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289603 13194139534462 true] [17592186289599 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289604 13194139534462 true] [17592186289599 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534462 true] [17592186289599 1041 "selection" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289599 1104 #edb/ref 17592186289601 13194139534462 true] [17592186289599 1110 #uuid "c8c9a23e-330d-59db-ab11-90c929b1ea8f" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289600 1034 "$A$ and $C$ only" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289600 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534462 true] [17592186289600 1111 #uuid "cf34da00-3c9c-5045-9857-9289dbf189d6" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289601 1034 "$D$ only" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289601 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534462 true] [17592186289601 1111 #uuid "df016bc0-b4ee-56d2-83d6-7e63864a8d91" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289602 1034 "$B$ and $C$ only" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289602 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534462 true] [17592186289602 1111 #uuid "ee65c5e2-2e0c-5a42-9345-ef5a410d0027" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289603 1034 "$C$ only" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289603 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534462 true] [17592186289603 1111 #uuid "53172910-3f6a-5916-bcfa-b58b71665829" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289604 1034 "$B$ only" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289604 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534462 true] [17592186289604 1111 #uuid "9121759a-0d14-5a06-bde3-3aba59bb816e" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289605 1017 #edb/ref 17592186289606 13194139534462 true] [17592186289605 1089 "q-73401" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289605 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534462 true] [17592186289605 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011175/assets/48569328379c72215884b759687c7c40ced06f9864d05d2638e4389694f90630.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. What are the $x$-coordinates of the stationary points?" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289605 1113 #uuid "c043bdf3-7728-5760-8821-193ed1e72bf3" 13194139534462 true] [17592186289605 1145 "A stationary point of $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.\n\nIn the given graph, we can see that at $x=0,$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011175/assets/576d25a420f1f4ebcbd0e9b055d7a27159caa8f1721ff5f35a32b86c3e3a0fb3.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, there is only one stationary point, and its $x$-coordinate is $x=0.$\n\n**Note:** At $x=2$, there is a sharp corner, so ${f}^{′}(2)$ doesn't exist. This gives one more critical point. But this is not a stationary point." 13194139534462 true] [17592186289606 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289607 13194139534462 true] [17592186289606 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289608 13194139534462 true] [17592186289606 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289609 13194139534462 true] [17592186289606 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289610 13194139534462 true] [17592186289606 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289611 13194139534462 true] [17592186289606 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534462 true] [17592186289606 1041 "selection" 13194139